30,2,,status,Kenji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Mizuki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"It seems our previous ""specialized"" projects were disappointments. The community values flexibility and general-purpose access over niche facilities.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,My findings are concerning. We often ignore the cost of displaced classes. The timetable cannot absorb these losses without expensive off-site rentals.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"How delightful. It appears we have a habit of scheduling outdoor work during the rainy season and dreaming of ""comfort"" projects that never end.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Guys, the numbers are way off! The actual tender prices for plant equipment are much higher than what we've been budgeting for.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"While you're shocked by the bills, I've found that our technicians are wasting hours on manual tasks because the equipment is outdated.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, that's a strong point. If we upgrade the equipment, we solve both the staff burden and the utility issue.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"But we must be careful. If the upgrade requires taking rooms out of service, we risk another timetable crisis.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, that depends on the scope. If we choose a project with a tight, indoor-focused delivery, we avoid the weather-induced delays I've seen.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Kenji,,And we can't forget that we have no money set aside for AV replacements. Any plan that adds new tech needs a long-term funding strategy.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, the ""cost"" of doing nothing is the burnout of our remaining staff. Manual routines are a failure of management, not just budget.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,Let's look for a candidate that balances these. We need something that improves staff efficiency but remains accessible to multiple users.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,I strongly suggest we avoid any plan that closes a high-demand general room. The booking data shows we are already at capacity.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"I'm leaning toward the most conservative schedule. I have no appetite for another project that ""squeezes"" a year of work into three weeks.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I'll support any plan that is realistically priced. I'm tired of seeing ""approved"" figures that are basically fairy tales.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"I'll support the plan that removes the most manual labor. I'm not interested in ""flexibility"" if the technicians are still scrubbing floors by hand.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I'll keep the user satisfaction data in mind. We shouldn't build a ""perfect"" lab that only three people can use.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"Exactly. Efficiency is not just about the equipment, but about how the space serves the whole university's schedule.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,Logic suggests we prioritize the project with the lowest risk of external delay. Indoor renovations are far safer than new extensions.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"As long as the budget includes a buffer for the actual market rates, I'm on board with a safe, indoor project.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,As long as it isn't just a fresh coat of paint. We need functional upgrades that actually reduce the daily grind.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
